WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.500
In this problem, a given area is being graded for a project.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.800
How much additional film material in cubic yards will be required at or need to be removed

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.500
from this project site.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:20.300
So when I see a problem like this, the words that come to mind for me are cut and fill.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.940
Searching for either of those in the reference manual will bring us to the Earthwork Volumes

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:31.240
section of chapter 2, where we can find the average end area method described for

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.740
finding Earthwork, Cut, and Fill volumes.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:41.420
The average end area method for Earthwork calculates volume between two consecutive cross

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:46.780
sections as the average of their areas multiplied by the distance between them, where

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.540
fill is positive and cut is negative.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.640
The equation is given as follows.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:02.240
The volume here equals the multiplication of the distance between two cross sections L,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:12.439
and the sum of their end areas of cross section A1 and A2 divided by two.

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:15.040
So let's begin to solve this problem.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:21.920
We have already been given our table of stations as well as the cut and fill volumes being

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.359
excavated or cut and fill areas being excavated at each station.

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:38.000
So plugging these into the equation, average end area method should give us what we need.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:48.000
So first we're going to go from station 1 to station 2.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:04.280
And then we're going to go from station 2 to station 3.50.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:14.280
Next we can focus on our cut volumes first.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:25.599
So in this case V equals our length between stations is 100 feet, and we have no cut volumes

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:28.360
in area 1 or area 2.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:40.520
So this is going to simply be 0 cubic feet.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:53.200
We do have a cut volume in area 2 of the measurement between station 2 and 3.5.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.320
So the length here is 150 feet between the two stations.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.040
And again area 1 for us, which is the old area 2, will be 0.

00:03:05.039 --> 00:03:12.719
Area 2 for our current one is going to be 50.

00:03:12.719 --> 00:03:26.479
And this will give us 37.50 feet cubed.

00:03:26.479 --> 00:03:36.079
Next we can look at our fill volumes for both stations.

00:03:36.079 --> 00:03:42.919
Again our total length between station 1 and 2 is 100 feet.

00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:51.239
And this time we have fill values in both of these stations.

00:03:51.239 --> 00:04:04.039
200 feet squared plus 75 feet squared over 2.

00:04:04.039 --> 00:04:15.120
And that's going to give us 13, 750 feet cubed.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:21.319
And our next measurement is going to be between station 2 and 3.5.

00:04:21.319 --> 00:04:26.159
I keep saying that wrong, but between these two stations.

00:04:26.159 --> 00:04:30.879
So that's going to be 150 feet.

00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:35.680
This is going to be whoops not 50.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:46.759
This is going to be 75 feet squared plus 0 feet squared over 2.

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:55.719
And that's going to give us 5, 6, 2, 5 feet cubed.

00:04:55.719 --> 00:04:59.920
So now we've assembled our total cut volumes for each station.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.120
And our total fill volumes for each station.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.800
So our next step is to simply sum all of these together.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:13.439
The formula notes that fill is positive and cut is negative.

00:05:13.439 --> 00:05:16.400
So let's keep that convention here.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:29.800
So we have plus 13, 750 feet cubed for the fill volume for station 1 to 2.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:40.960
And plus 5, 6, 2, 5 feet cubed from station 2 to 3 plus 50.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:51.879
And nothing in the fill volume, the cut volume for station 1 to 2.

00:05:51.879 --> 00:06:06.040
And then negative 3, 750 feet cubed.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:12.800
So fill, fill, cut, cut.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:28.800
And that's going to come out to 15, 6, 2, 5 feet cubed of fill material.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:36.000
And finally, since the equation is asking, or the question is asking for this to be in cubic yards.

00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:38.959
We will convert that.

00:06:38.959 --> 00:07:04.319
So 15, 6, 2, 5 feet cubed times 1 yard cubed over 3 feet cubed equals 578 yards cubed of fill material.

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:14.719
So as you can see here, it's going to require approximately 580 additional cubic yards of fill material in order to complete this project.

00:07:14.719 --> 00:07:29.480
Logically, we can even see this in the image based on the fact that station 1 and station 2 both require more fill material than can possibly even be excavated at the third station.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:38.680
So this whole problem pretty much only requires knowing where to find the average end area method equation and how to apply it, which is pretty simple.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.240
And that's all it takes to solve this problem.
